lang: c
name: C
ext: c
mode: browser
runtime: picoc (a C interpreter) compiled to WebAssembly; every kata also exports a gcc self-test
run: cc kata.c && ./a.out
blurb: Pointers, bytes and bit twiddling: the C you need to read malware and write exploits. Each kata is one function; the test harness calls it.
notes: The in-browser runtime is picoc, so a few corners of C aren't there: function pointers, pointer subtraction, anonymous structs, and several fields declared on one line inside a struct. The katas stay clear of them. Use [ self-test ] to run the exact same tests under gcc.

=== popcount
title: Count the set bits
level: 1
tags: bits
ctype: int
prompt:
  Write `int popcount(unsigned int x)` that returns how many bits are 1 in `x`. No builtins like `__builtin_popcount`.
starter:
  int popcount(unsigned int x) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  int popcount(unsigned int x) {
      int n = 0;
      while (x) {
          x &= x - 1;   /* clears the lowest set bit */
          n++;
      }
      return n;
  }
hints:
  - Check `x & 1`, then shift right with `x >>= 1`, until `x` is zero.
  - Faster trick: `x &= x - 1` clears the lowest set bit, so the loop runs once per 1-bit.
test: zero :: popcount(0) :: 0
test: one bit :: popcount(8) :: 1
test: a byte of ones :: popcount(0xFF) :: 8
test: all 32 bits :: popcount(0xFFFFFFFF) :: 32
test: 0xDEADBEEF :: popcount(0xDEADBEEF) :: 24

=== my-strlen
title: strlen without strlen
level: 1
tags: strings, pointers
ctype: int
prompt:
  Write `int my_strlen(char *s)` that counts characters up to (not including) the NUL terminator. Walk the string with a pointer, no `<string.h>`.
starter:
  int my_strlen(char *s) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  int my_strlen(char *s) {
      int n = 0;
      while (*s++)
          n++;
      return n;
  }
hints:
  - A C string ends at the byte `'\0'`. Loop until `*s == 0`.
test: empty :: my_strlen("") :: 0
test: hello :: my_strlen("hello") :: 5
test: with spaces :: my_strlen("0xDay dojo") :: 10

=== reverse
title: Reverse a string in place
level: 2
tags: strings, pointers
ctype: str
prompt:
  Write `void reverse(char *s)` that reverses the string in its own buffer, no second buffer. Swap from both ends toward the middle.
support:
  char buf_[64];
  char *rev(char *s) { strcpy(buf_, s); reverse(buf_); return buf_; }
starter:
  void reverse(char *s) {
  }
solution:
  void reverse(char *s) {
      int i = 0, j = strlen(s) - 1;
      while (i < j) {
          char t = s[i];
          s[i] = s[j];
          s[j] = t;
          i++;
          j--;
      }
  }
hints:
  - Two indexes: `i = 0` and `j = strlen(s) - 1`. Swap, move both inward, stop when they meet.
test: odd length :: rev("abc") :: "cba"
test: even length :: rev("abcd") :: "dcba"
test: one char :: rev("x") :: "x"
test: empty :: rev("") :: ""
test: sentence :: rev("live on time") :: "emit no evil"

=== bswap
title: Swap endianness
level: 2
tags: bits, endianness
ctype: hex
prompt:
  x86 is little-endian, network byte order is big-endian. Write `unsigned int bswap32(unsigned int x)` that reverses the byte order: `0x12345678` becomes `0x78563412`. Shifts and masks only.
starter:
  unsigned int bswap32(unsigned int x) {
      return x;
  }
solution:
  unsigned int bswap32(unsigned int x) {
      return ((x >> 24) & 0x000000FF) |
             ((x >>  8) & 0x0000FF00) |
             ((x <<  8) & 0x00FF0000) |
             ((x << 24) & 0xFF000000);
  }
hints:
  - Byte 3 moves to byte 0: `(x >> 24) & 0xFF`. Work out the other three the same way.
test: textbook :: bswap32(0x12345678) :: 0x78563412
test: dead beef :: bswap32(0xDEADBEEF) :: 0xEFBEADDE
test: twice is identity :: bswap32(bswap32(0xCAFEBABE)) :: 0xCAFEBABE
test: low byte goes high :: bswap32(0x000000FF) :: 0xFF000000

=== palindrome
title: Palindrome, ignoring junk
level: 2
tags: strings
ctype: int
prompt:
  Write `int is_palindrome(char *s)` that returns 1 if `s` reads the same both ways when you skip anything that isn't a letter or digit and ignore case, else 0. `"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"` is a palindrome.
starter:
  int is_palindrome(char *s) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  int is_palindrome(char *s) {
      int i = 0, j = strlen(s) - 1;
      while (i < j) {
          if (!isalnum(s[i])) { i++; continue; }
          if (!isalnum(s[j])) { j--; continue; }
          if (tolower(s[i]) != tolower(s[j]))
              return 0;
          i++;
          j--;
      }
      return 1;
  }
hints:
  - Two pointers again. Skip forward or backward past anything `isalnum()` rejects.
  - Compare `tolower(s[i])` with `tolower(s[j])`.
test: classic :: is_palindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama") :: 1
test: not one :: is_palindrome("0xDay") :: 0
test: digits count :: is_palindrome("12:21") :: 1
test: empty is a palindrome :: is_palindrome("") :: 1
test: only junk :: is_palindrome("?!.") :: 1

=== ip-to-u32
title: Parse an IPv4 address
level: 3
tags: parsing, networking
ctype: hex
prompt:
  Write `unsigned int ip_to_u32(char *ip)` that turns dotted-quad text like `"192.168.1.10"` into `0xC0A8010A`. Parse the digits yourself (no `inet_aton`, no `sscanf`). Return `0xFFFFFFFF` if there aren't exactly four parts or any part is over 255.
starter:
  unsigned int ip_to_u32(char *ip) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  unsigned int ip_to_u32(char *ip) {
      unsigned int result = 0;
      int parts = 0, val = 0, digits = 0;
      while (1) {
          char c = *ip;
          if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {
              val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
              digits++;
              if (val > 255) return 0xFFFFFFFF;
          } else if (c == '.' || c == '\0') {
              if (digits == 0) return 0xFFFFFFFF;
              result = (result << 8) | val;
              parts++;
              val = 0;
              digits = 0;
              if (c == '\0') break;
          } else {
              return 0xFFFFFFFF;
          }
          ip++;
      }
      return parts == 4 ? result : 0xFFFFFFFF;
  }
hints:
  - Build each octet with `val = val * 10 + (c - '0')`.
  - At each `.` (and at the final `\0`), shift the result left 8 and OR the octet in.
test: private address :: ip_to_u32("192.168.1.10") :: 0xC0A8010A
test: zeros :: ip_to_u32("0.0.0.0") :: 0
test: broadcast :: ip_to_u32("255.255.255.255") :: 0xFFFFFFFF
test: loopback :: ip_to_u32("127.0.0.1") :: 0x7F000001
test: octet over 255 :: ip_to_u32("10.0.0.256") :: 0xFFFFFFFF
test: too few parts :: ip_to_u32("10.0.1") :: 0xFFFFFFFF
test: empty part :: ip_to_u32("10..0.1") :: 0xFFFFFFFF

=== adler32
title: Adler-32 checksum
level: 3
tags: checksums, bytes
ctype: hex
prompt:
  zlib uses Adler-32. Keep two 16-bit sums mod 65521: `a` starts at 1 and adds each byte, `b` starts at 0 and adds `a` after every byte. The result is `(b << 16) | a`. Write `unsigned int adler32(char *data, int len)`. Treat bytes as unsigned.
starter:
  unsigned int adler32(char *data, int len) {
      return 1;
  }
solution:
  unsigned int adler32(char *data, int len) {
      unsigned int a = 1, b = 0;
      int i;
      for (i = 0; i < len; i++) {
          a = (a + (unsigned char)data[i]) % 65521;
          b = (b + a) % 65521;
      }
      return (b << 16) | a;
  }
hints:
  - Cast each byte with `(unsigned char)data[i]` so bytes above 0x7F don't go negative.
test: empty input :: adler32("", 0) :: 0x00000001
test: Wikipedia :: adler32("Wikipedia", 9) :: 0x11E60398
test: high bytes :: adler32("\xff\xfe", 2) :: 0x02FE01FE

=== atoi
title: atoi, but careful
level: 3
tags: parsing
ctype: int
prompt:
  Write `int my_atoi(char *s)`: skip leading spaces, accept one optional `+` or `-`, then read digits until the first non-digit. No digits means 0. You don't need to handle overflow.
starter:
  int my_atoi(char *s) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  int my_atoi(char *s) {
      int sign = 1, n = 0;
      while (*s == ' ' || *s == '\t')
          s++;
      if (*s == '+' || *s == '-') {
          if (*s == '-') sign = -1;
          s++;
      }
      while (*s >= '0' && *s <= '9') {
          n = n * 10 + (*s - '0');
          s++;
      }
      return sign * n;
  }
hints:
  - Three phases: skip whitespace, read a sign, read digits.
test: plain :: my_atoi("1337") :: 1337
test: negative :: my_atoi("-42") :: -42
test: leading spaces and plus :: my_atoi("   +7") :: 7
test: stops at junk :: my_atoi("80/tcp") :: 80
test: no digits :: my_atoi("abc") :: 0

=== crc32
title: CRC-32, bit by bit
level: 4
tags: checksums, bits
ctype: hex
prompt:
  Write `unsigned int crc32(char *data, int len)` for the standard CRC-32 used by zip, PNG and Ethernet. Start with `crc = 0xFFFFFFFF`. For each byte, XOR it into the low byte of `crc`, then 8 times: if the low bit is set, `crc = (crc >> 1) ^ 0xEDB88320`, else `crc >>= 1`. Return `~crc` at the end.
starter:
  unsigned int crc32(char *data, int len) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  unsigned int crc32(char *data, int len) {
      unsigned int crc = 0xFFFFFFFF;
      int i, k;
      for (i = 0; i < len; i++) {
          crc ^= (unsigned char)data[i];
          for (k = 0; k < 8; k++) {
              if (crc & 1)
                  crc = (crc >> 1) ^ 0xEDB88320;
              else
                  crc = crc >> 1;
          }
      }
      return ~crc;
  }
hints:
  - Keep `crc` unsigned so `>>` shifts in zeros.
  - The magic constant `0xEDB88320` is the reversed polynomial 0x04C11DB7.
test: empty :: crc32("", 0) :: 0x00000000
test: check value :: crc32("123456789", 9) :: 0xCBF43926
test: quick brown fox :: crc32("The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog", 43) :: 0x414FA339

=== brackets
title: Balanced brackets
level: 4
tags: stacks
ctype: int
prompt:
  Write `int balanced(char *s)` that returns 1 if every `(`, `[` and `{` is closed by the matching bracket in the right order, else 0. Ignore other characters. Inputs are under 256 characters, so a fixed-size array works as the stack.
starter:
  int balanced(char *s) {
      return 0;
  }
solution:
  int balanced(char *s) {
      char stack[256];
      int top = 0;
      for (; *s; s++) {
          char c = *s;
          if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {
              stack[top++] = c;
          } else if (c == ')' || c == ']' || c == '}') {
              char want = c == ')' ? '(' : c == ']' ? '[' : '{';
              if (top == 0 || stack[top - 1] != want)
                  return 0;
              top--;
          }
      }
      return top == 0;
  }
hints:
  - Push openers. On a closer, the top of the stack must be its partner; pop it.
  - At the end the stack has to be empty.
test: simple :: balanced("()") :: 1
test: nested :: balanced("{[()()]}") :: 1
test: wrong order :: balanced("([)]") :: 0
test: unclosed :: balanced("((") :: 0
test: stray closer :: balanced("())") :: 0
test: code with junk :: balanced("int main() { return a[0]; }") :: 1

=== linked-list
title: Reverse a linked list
level: 4
tags: pointers, structs, malloc
ctype: str
prompt:
  Given `struct node { int v; struct node *next; };`, write `struct node *reverse_list(struct node *head)` that reverses the list by relinking the nodes (no new allocations) and returns the new head. The harness builds lists with `malloc` and prints them as `"1>2>3"`.
support:
  struct node *build(int n) {
      struct node *head = NULL;
      int i;
      for (i = n; i >= 1; i--) {
          struct node *x = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
          x->v = i;
          x->next = head;
          head = x;
      }
      return head;
  }
  char out_[128];
  char *show(struct node *h) {
      char tmp[16];
      out_[0] = 0;
      while (h != NULL) {
          sprintf(tmp, "%d", h->v);
          strcat(out_, tmp);
          if (h->next != NULL) strcat(out_, ">");
          h = h->next;
      }
      return out_;
  }
  char *rev_n(int n) { return show(reverse_list(build(n))); }
starter:
  struct node {
      int v;
      struct node *next;
  };

  struct node *reverse_list(struct node *head) {
      return head;
  }
solution:
  struct node {
      int v;
      struct node *next;
  };

  struct node *reverse_list(struct node *head) {
      struct node *prev = NULL;
      while (head != NULL) {
          struct node *next = head->next;
          head->next = prev;
          prev = head;
          head = next;
      }
      return prev;
  }
hints:
  - Keep three pointers: `prev`, `head` and the saved `next`.
  - Each step: save `head->next`, point `head->next` at `prev`, then slide both forward.
test: three nodes :: rev_n(3) :: "3>2>1"
test: one node :: rev_n(1) :: "1"
test: empty list :: rev_n(0) :: ""
test: five nodes :: rev_n(5) :: "5>4>3>2>1"

=== b64-c
title: Base64 encode in C
level: 5
tags: encoding, bits, buffers
ctype: str
prompt:
  Write `void b64(unsigned char *in, int len, char *out)` that writes the Base64 encoding of `len` bytes into `out` (with `=` padding) and NUL-terminates it. The caller guarantees `out` is big enough.
support:
  char o_[128];
  char *enc(char *s) { b64((unsigned char *)s, strlen(s), o_); return o_; }
starter:
  char *T = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/";

  void b64(unsigned char *in, int len, char *out) {
      out[0] = 0;
  }
solution:
  char *T = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/";

  void b64(unsigned char *in, int len, char *out) {
      int i, o = 0;
      for (i = 0; i < len; i += 3) {
          unsigned int n = in[i] << 16;
          if (i + 1 < len) n |= in[i + 1] << 8;
          if (i + 2 < len) n |= in[i + 2];
          out[o++] = T[(n >> 18) & 63];
          out[o++] = T[(n >> 12) & 63];
          out[o++] = i + 1 < len ? T[(n >> 6) & 63] : '=';
          out[o++] = i + 2 < len ? T[n & 63] : '=';
      }
      out[o] = 0;
  }
hints:
  - Same plan as the Python kata: 3 bytes into a 24-bit `unsigned int`, then four 6-bit indexes.
  - Only OR in the second and third bytes if they exist; emit `=` for the missing ones.
test: Man :: enc("Man") :: "TWFu"
test: one pad :: enc("Ma") :: "TWE="
test: two pads :: enc("M") :: "TQ=="
test: empty :: enc("") :: ""
test: longer :: enc("0xDay dojo") :: "MHhEYXkgZG9qbw=="
